      "abstract": "<jats:p>This paper presents a systematic study of simple continued fractions and their fundamental properties. It begins by introducing the basic definitions, notation, and classification of finite and infinite continued fractions. The expansion of both rational and irrational numbers into continued fractions is developed, together with proofs establishing the existence and uniqueness of such representations. Particular attention is given to quadratic irrational numbers and their periodic continued fraction expansions. The theory of convergents is then explored through recurrence relations and their fundamental properties, demonstrating how convergents provide increasingly accurate rational approximations of real numbers. Finally, the paper investigates the theory of best rational approximations, proving the approximation properties of convergents and presenting Legendre's Theorem as a criterion for identifying convergents. Throughout the work, illustrative examples accompany the theoretical development to demonstrate the computational aspects and applications of continued fractions in number theory.</jats:p>",
